WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.696
This is Chapter One, Episode 1, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.246 --> 00:00:18.159
Now we come to the next big turning point: the Reformation, the Swedish wars, and the introduction of absolute monarchy, roughly fifteen hundred to seventeen hundred.

4
00:00:18.108 --> 00:00:23.546
With the Reformation in 1536, Protestantism was introduced in Denmark.

5
00:00:23.546 --> 00:00:27.608
At the same time, the king's power became much greater.

6
00:00:27.608 --> 00:00:33.762
The church was placed under the king, instead of under the Pope, and the king took over the church's property.

7
00:00:34.312 --> 00:00:41.900
In the Middle Ages, the Danish church was part of the Catholic church, which belonged directly under the Pope in Rome.

8
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.700
So the church in Denmark was relatively independent of the king.

9
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.812
It was a rich and powerful institution.

10
00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:55.325
But around the year 1500 its power was threatened by several movements that wanted to reform the church.

11
00:00:55.325 --> 00:00:57.100
They were called Protestant.

12
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.387
One of them was led by the German monk Martin Luther.

13
00:01:00.387 --> 00:01:04.450
The revolt led large parts of Northern Europe to break with the Pope.

14
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:11.508
Instead, independent Protestant churches were founded in close cooperation with local kings and princes.

15
00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:15.921
Martin Luther's ideas came to Denmark in the 1520s.

16
00:01:15.871 --> 00:01:23.021
It was especially the priest Hans Tausen, who lived from fourteen ninety-four to fifteen sixty-one, who spread them.

17
00:01:23.021 --> 00:01:30.321
Their central point was that you could only achieve salvation by believing, not by doing good deeds or by buying indulgences.

18
00:01:30.321 --> 00:01:37.734
In several Danish towns the discussions grew into popular resistance against the rich and powerful Catholic church.

19
00:01:38.284 --> 00:01:46.347
In 1534 civil war broke out in Denmark between the Catholic leaders of the church and the supporters of Protestantism.

20
00:01:46.297 --> 00:01:54.747
Under the leadership of Christian the Third, who reigned from fifteen thirty-six to fifty-nine, the Protestants won in 1536.

21
00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:58.734
The same year, the Reformation was formally carried out.

22
00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:03.872
Denmark became a Protestant country, and the king removed the Catholic bishops.

23
00:02:03.872 --> 00:02:07.509
The Danish church now belonged to the king instead of the Pope.

24
00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:10.872
And the church's political power became much smaller.

25
00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:18.234
The Catholic church had owned very large values and a lot of land, and now the king took all of it over.

26
00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:21.772
At the same time, he closed the monasteries.

27
00:02:21.772 --> 00:02:25.984
Until then the language of the church had been Latin, which few understood.

28
00:02:25.984 --> 00:02:31.135
Martin Luther believed everyone should have access to Christianity in their own language.

29
00:02:31.135 --> 00:02:39.160
So the Reformation meant that the Bible was translated into Danish, and church services and hymn singing from then on took place only in Danish.

30
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:45.834
"Christian the Third's Bible", the first translation of the Bible into Danish, came out in 1550.

31
00:02:46.384 --> 00:02:51.184
The Reformation also meant that only the king could appoint priests and bishops.

32
00:02:51.134 --> 00:03:02.084
In this way the church could be used to strengthen royal power, and, with the translated Bible, to spread a common Danish written language to every corner of the kingdom.

33
00:03:02.084 --> 00:03:13.221
In the longer term, the Reformation was of decisive importance for Danish history, because it tied state, church, language and population closely together.

34
00:03:13.221 --> 00:03:22.140
In the short term, it became the beginning of a strong state power, which in 1660 to 61 developed into royal absolutism.

35
00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:30.177
Now, a king who left a huge mark: Christian the Fourth, who reigned from fifteen eighty-eight to sixteen forty-eight.

36
00:03:30.127 --> 00:03:38.565
He was king for almost sixty years, longer than anyone else in Danish history, and he is one of the most significant kings.

37
00:03:38.565 --> 00:03:52.490
Like other kings of his time, he had a great interest in art and architecture, and was behind a long series of buildings, especially in and around the capital, Copenhagen, in the first half of the 1600s.

38
00:03:52.490 --> 00:04:04.302
Among the well-known buildings he had built: Frederiksborg Castle at Hillerød, the Copenhagen Stock Exchange, Rosenborg Castle, and the Round Tower, Rundetårn, in Copenhagen.

39
00:04:04.302 --> 00:04:17.166
He also founded several new towns, mostly outside present-day Denmark: Kristianstad in Skåne, Kristiansand in Norway, and Kristiania, which is now called Oslo, the capital of Norway.

40
00:04:17.716 --> 00:04:22.654
But Christian the Fourth's reign was also marked by lost wars and lost land.

41
00:04:22.603 --> 00:04:29.779
When he took over the government, Denmark dominated the Baltic area, but Sweden also had big ambitions there.

42
00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:44.328
The balance of power changed markedly when, in the years sixteen twenty-five to twenty-nine, Christian pulled Denmark into the Thirty Years' War, a great European war that was mainly fought in Germany, from sixteen eighteen to forty-eight.

43
00:04:44.328 --> 00:04:56.088
The king suffered a big military defeat in 1626, and when Denmark withdrew from the war in 1629, Sweden had become the leading power in the Baltic area.

44
00:04:56.638 --> 00:05:01.800
The poor relationship with Sweden continued for the rest of Christian the Fourth's reign.

45
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:16.725
In 1643 Denmark was attacked by Sweden, and in 1645 Denmark had to give up, among other things, the province of Halland for thirty years, the island of Gotland in the Baltic Sea, and parts of Norway to Sweden.

46
00:05:16.725 --> 00:05:27.402
When Christian the Fourth died in 1648, Denmark was a country in deep crisis, and Sweden's superiority would last for the rest of the 1600s.

47
00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:34.827
In the years sixteen fifty-seven to sixty, Denmark was at war with Sweden again, in two rounds.

48
00:05:34.777 --> 00:05:41.014
In the first, Swedish troops had marched up from a war in Poland and occupied Jutland.

49
00:05:41.014 --> 00:05:48.827
Then came the extremely cold winter of sixteen fifty-seven to fifty-eight, when a thick layer of ice formed on the sea around Denmark.

50
00:05:48.827 --> 00:05:53.027
The Danish fleet was therefore cut off from defending the Danish islands.

51
00:05:53.027 --> 00:06:03.132
The Swedes, on the other hand, could walk across the ice to Funen, and then to Langeland, Lolland and Zealand, and they conquered large parts of Denmark.

52
00:06:03.682 --> 00:06:16.294
At the peace treaty in Roskilde, the Peace of Roskilde, in 1658, Denmark had to give up Skåne, Halland and Blekinge for good, and several Norwegian areas, to Sweden.

53
00:06:16.244 --> 00:06:19.507
Bornholm was also ceded to Sweden in the same treaty.

54
00:06:19.507 --> 00:06:27.774
But the island's population quickly staged an uprising and overthrew the Swedish rule, so that Bornholm became Danish again.

55
00:06:28.324 --> 00:06:34.074
Later the same year, the Swedish king regretted not having conquered all of Denmark.

56
00:06:34.024 --> 00:06:40.961
War came again, and the Swedes besieged Copenhagen, which means they surrounded the city to make it surrender.

57
00:06:40.961 --> 00:06:49.374
But with help from the Netherlands, the Danish capital managed to withstand the siege, and the Swedes did not conquer more Danish areas.

58
00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:52.680
They also had to accept that Bornholm would remain Danish.

59
00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:57.180
But losing so much land had big consequences for Denmark.

60
00:06:57.130 --> 00:07:04.767
A large loss of population, of fertile farmland, of valuable trade, and of a long coastline on the Baltic Sea.

61
00:07:04.767 --> 00:07:12.942
And Denmark no longer controlled both sides of the Øresund, the Sound, which is the entrance and exit of the Baltic Sea.

62
00:07:12.942 --> 00:07:17.322
After that, Denmark was no longer a power in Northern Europe.

63
00:07:17.872 --> 00:07:21.097
And this is how the stage was set for the great change.

64
00:07:21.047 --> 00:07:30.422
After the lost wars to Sweden and the country's poor economy, the demand grew among the population to abolish the special privileges of the nobility.

65
00:07:30.422 --> 00:07:34.347
The nobility made up less than one percent of the population.

66
00:07:34.347 --> 00:07:39.222
They were magnates, typically landowners, and they had a series of privileges.

67
00:07:39.222 --> 00:07:42.334
For example, they did not have to pay tax.

68
00:07:42.334 --> 00:07:45.259
They could choose who would be the new king when a king died.

69
00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:49.484
And with a håndfæstning they could force the new king to share power with them.

70
00:07:49.484 --> 00:07:55.116
In return for their privileges, they had to give military support to the king in time of war.

71
00:07:55.666 --> 00:08:09.778
In October 1660, King Frederik the Third, who reigned from sixteen forty-eight to seventy, introduced that the nobility would from now on pay tax, among other things to rebuild the country after the wars against Sweden.

72
00:08:09.728 --> 00:08:20.142
The nobility, which had not been much help during the war, also had to accept that royal power would from now on automatically be inherited from the reigning king to his nearest heir.

73
00:08:20.692 --> 00:08:30.129
The king used his new power, in sixteen sixty to sixty-one, to introduce a new form of government in Denmark, called absolutism, "enevælde".

74
00:08:30.079 --> 00:08:33.317
The king now governed the country completely alone.

75
00:08:33.317 --> 00:08:40.104
He could appoint officials, introduce laws and collect taxes without first asking anyone's permission.

76
00:08:40.104 --> 00:08:45.840
A real central state administration emerged, and a permanent army controlled by the king.

77
00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:54.940
And as the only absolute monarchy in Europe, Denmark got in 1665 a constitution that was formulated and written down.

78
00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.928
This law was called the King's Law, Kongeloven.

79
00:08:57.928 --> 00:09:03.553
It established, first and foremost, that the king had almost unlimited power.

80
00:09:03.553 --> 00:09:06.928
It set only very few limits on what the king could do.

81
00:09:06.928 --> 00:09:11.577
He was not allowed to give up his absolute power, neither wholly nor partly.

82
00:09:11.577 --> 00:09:18.440
He was also not allowed to divide the kingdom between his children, but had to let everything pass to the heir to the throne.

83
00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:27.522
And finally, he had to belong to the Protestant church, which had been created with the Reformation in the 1500s, based on Martin Luther's teaching.

84
00:09:28.072 --> 00:09:36.885
The King's Law remained the foundation of the king's absolute power all the way until the first democratic constitution in 1849.

85
00:09:36.834 --> 00:09:40.500
And that is exactly where we pick up the story in Episode Two.

86
00:09:41.092 --> 00:09:44.617
Before we go, here is the quick recap for your exam.

87
00:09:44.567 --> 00:09:48.992
Denmark has existed as an independent state for more than a thousand years.

88
00:09:48.992 --> 00:09:59.179
Harald Blåtand, around 965, is regarded as the first king of all Denmark, and the Jelling stone is Denmark's baptismal certificate.

89
00:09:59.179 --> 00:10:03.392
Odin, Thor and Freja are gods of the old Asatro religion.

90
00:10:03.392 --> 00:10:07.292
Svend Tveskæg conquered England in 1013.

91
00:10:07.292 --> 00:10:12.892
About three thousand churches had been built by 1250, in the Catholic Middle Ages.

92
00:10:12.892 --> 00:10:17.392
Margrete the First created the Kalmar Union in 1397.

93
00:10:17.392 --> 00:10:20.854
Christian the Fourth built Rosenborg and the Round Tower.

94
00:10:20.854 --> 00:10:33.692
The first håndfæstning was in 1282, the Jutland Law in 1241, the Black Death around 1350, and the Kalmar Union in 1397, dissolved in 1523.

95
00:10:33.692 --> 00:10:46.130
The Reformation came in 1536, Christian the Third's Bible in 1550, and the Peace of Roskilde in 1658, which gave Skåne, Halland and Blekinge to Sweden.

96
00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:52.130
The Reformation gave the king power over the church, and absolutism gave the king even more power.

97
00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:58.630
Absolutism began in 1660 to 61, and the King's Law dates from 1665.

98
00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.384
See you in Episode Two.

99
00:11:00.934 --> 00:11:03.534
Now it is time for your exam practice.

100
00:11:03.484 --> 00:11:08.397
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

101
00:11:08.397 --> 00:11:15.882
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

102
00:11:16.432 --> 00:11:17.682
Question 1.

103
00:11:17.632 --> 00:11:23.832
How did the relationship between the king and the church change as a result of the Reformation in the 1500s?

104
00:11:23.832 --> 00:11:27.570
Option A: The king got less power over the church.

105
00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:31.236
Option B: The king got more power over the church.
[DA] Hvordan ændrede forholdet mellem kongen og kirken sig som følge af reformationen i 1500-tallet?  A: Kongen fik mindre magt over kirken  |  B: Kongen fik mere magt over kirken

106
00:11:37.786 --> 00:11:41.686
The answer is B: The king got more power over the church.

107
00:11:41.636 --> 00:11:44.286
It appeared in the May 2023 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Kongen fik mere magt over kirken

108
00:11:44.836 --> 00:11:46.086
Question 2.

109
00:11:46.036 --> 00:11:49.736
What did the Reformation in Denmark in 1536 involve?

110
00:11:49.736 --> 00:11:53.786
Option A: Introduction of a democratic constitution.

111
00:11:53.786 --> 00:11:58.536
Option B: The transition from Catholic to Protestant Christianity.

112
00:11:58.536 --> 00:12:04.104
Option C: Abolition of the king's exclusive right to trade with foreign countries.
[DA] Hvad indebar reformationen i Danmark i 1536?  A: Indførelsen af en demokratisk grundlov  |  B: Overgangen fra katolsk til protestantisk kristendom  |  C: Ophævelse af kongens eneret på at handle med udlandet

113
00:12:10.654 --> 00:12:15.654
The answer is B: The transition from Catholic to Protestant Christianity.

114
00:12:15.604 --> 00:12:18.282
It appeared in the May 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Overgangen fra katolsk til protestantisk kristendom

115
00:12:18.832 --> 00:12:20.170
Question 3.

116
00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:26.832
Which king is behind the building of, among other things, the Round Tower and Rosenborg Castle in Copenhagen?

117
00:12:26.832 --> 00:12:29.395
Option A: Christian the Fourth.

118
00:12:29.395 --> 00:12:31.945
Option B: Christian the Seventh.

119
00:12:31.945 --> 00:12:34.380
Option C: Christian the Tenth.
[DA] Hvilken konge står bag opførelsen af blandt andet Rundetårn og Rosenborg Slot i København?  A: Christian den 4.  |  B: Christian den 7.  |  C: Christian den 10.

120
00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.680
The answer is A: Christian the Fourth.

121
00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.518
It appeared in the November 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Christian den 4.

122
00:12:47.068 --> 00:12:48.368
Question 4.

123
00:12:48.318 --> 00:12:52.193
Which wars involving Denmark were fought in the 1600s?

124
00:12:52.193 --> 00:12:54.843
Option A: The Schleswig Wars.

125
00:12:54.843 --> 00:12:57.306
Option B: The England Wars.

126
00:12:57.306 --> 00:12:59.952
Option C: The Swedish Wars.
[DA] Hvilke krige med dansk deltagelse blev udkæmpet i 1600-tallet?  A: De Slesvigske Krige  |  B: Englandskrigene  |  C: Svenskekrigene

127
00:13:06.502 --> 00:13:09.240
The answer is C: The Swedish Wars.

128
00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:13.794
It appeared in the November 2021 and November 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Svenskekrigene

129
00:13:14.344 --> 00:13:15.807
Question 5.

130
00:13:15.757 --> 00:13:20.619
Which territory did Denmark lose to Sweden in the middle of the 1600s?

131
00:13:20.619 --> 00:13:22.432
Option A: Halland.

132
00:13:22.432 --> 00:13:24.219
Option B: Småland.

133
00:13:24.219 --> 00:13:26.340
Option C: Värmland.
[DA] Hvilket af følgende landområder mistede Danmark til Sverige i midten af 1600-tallet?  A: Halland  |  B: Småland  |  C: Värmland

134
00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:34.853
The answer is A: Halland.

135
00:13:34.803 --> 00:13:37.422
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Halland

136
00:13:37.972 --> 00:13:39.410
Question 6.

137
00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:43.072
Which territories did Denmark lose in 1658?

138
00:13:43.072 --> 00:13:46.547
Option A: Skåne, Halland and Blekinge.

139
00:13:46.547 --> 00:13:49.735
Option B: The Shetland and Orkney Islands.

140
00:13:49.735 --> 00:13:52.728
Option C: The West Indian Islands.
[DA] Hvilke af følgende landområder mistede Danmark i 1658?  A: Skåne, Halland og Blekinge  |  B: Shetlandsøerne og Orkneyøerne  |  C: De Vestindiske Øer

141
00:13:59.278 --> 00:14:03.091
The answer is A: Skåne, Halland and Blekinge.

142
00:14:03.041 --> 00:14:05.826
It appeared in the June 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Skåne, Halland og Blekinge

143
00:14:06.376 --> 00:14:07.814
Question 7.

144
00:14:07.764 --> 00:14:12.289
What was the result of the war against Sweden in 1657 to 58?

145
00:14:12.289 --> 00:14:14.964
Option A: Denmark lost Skåne.

146
00:14:14.964 --> 00:14:17.916
Option B: Denmark conquered Skåne.
[DA] Hvad blev resultatet af krigen mod Sverige i 1657-58?  A: Danmark mistede Skåne  |  B: Danmark erobrede Skåne

147
00:14:24.466 --> 00:14:27.454
The answer is A: Denmark lost Skåne.

148
00:14:27.404 --> 00:14:30.318
It appeared in the June 2026 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Danmark mistede Skåne

149
00:14:30.868 --> 00:14:32.131
Question 8.

150
00:14:32.081 --> 00:14:38.031
Who gained more power with the absolute monarchy introduced in Denmark in 1660 to 61?

151
00:14:38.031 --> 00:14:40.343
Option A: The nobility.

152
00:14:40.343 --> 00:14:42.406
Option B: The church.

153
00:14:42.406 --> 00:14:44.448
Option C: The king.
[DA] Hvem fik mere magt med enevælden, der blev indført i Danmark i 1660-61?  A: Adelen  |  B: Kirken  |  C: Kongen

154
00:14:50.998 --> 00:14:53.161
The answer is C: The king.

155
00:14:53.111 --> 00:14:57.594
It appeared in the November 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Kongen

156
00:14:58.144 --> 00:14:59.907
That's the end of this part.

157
00:14:59.857 --> 00:15:00.924
Well done.

